WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.440
Barriers to 5G wireless networks are regulatory
– not technical.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:13.870
While the FCC is taking steps to modernize
existing deployment regulations,

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:17.500
most regulatory decisions about 5G
will be made at the local level.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:22.840
In California, San Jose and Sacramento
have taken vastly different approaches to 5G.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:28.540
San Jose sees 5G rollout as rent extraction, charging carriers $3,500

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.950
annually to access each of its street poles.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:35.570
Sacramento, on the other hand, is a model
city that has already launched its pilot 5G

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:41.059
program. It has 100 cell sites, expanded backhaul,
an expedited permitting process, and free

00:00:41.059 --> 00:00:42.859
Wi-Fi in 27 parks.

00:00:42.859 --> 00:00:47.019
Soon, Sacramento will experience wireless gigabit internet—speeds

00:00:47.019 --> 00:00:50.250
that were previously only available via fiber.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:55.469
Such forward-looking cities recognize that
private investment improves operational efficiency,

00:00:55.469 --> 00:00:57.560
lowers costs, and increases quality of life.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:02.200
Cities can reduce barriers to entry
will facilitate competition and choice.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:07.180
To stay ahead, American leaders
at all levels need to streamline the rollout of 5G.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.500
What are you most excited about in the coming 5G revolution?

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:15.360
Let us know in your comments. Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:22.500
to cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

